---
title: "Stuck"
description: "--<utility>--stuck: a value while the nearest sticky ancestor is stuck (experimental)."
section: Modifiers
sectionOrder: 2
order: 4
---

{% callout type="warning" title="Experimental" %}
Container scroll-state queries are native in Chromium only. Elsewhere `index.js` loads a small polyfill (`base/scroll-state.js`) that measures sticky containers on scroll and writes the stuck sides to `data-ui-stuck`. Markup is the same either way.
{% /callout %}

A sticky element (`--position: sticky`, or raw `position: sticky`) is a scroll-state container. While it is stuck to the side named by `--stuck-state` on it (`top` by default; also `right`, `bottom`, `left`, and the logical sides), `--<utility>--stuck` applies on its **descendants**: a container query never matches the container itself, so the tiers go on a child.

```html
<header style="--position: sticky; --position--lg: static; --top: 0; --z: 10">
  <div style="--bg: transparent; --bg--stuck: var(--color-background); --shadow--stuck: var(--shadow-md)">
    …
  </div>
</header>
```

`stuck` ranks below every other state (`--bg--hover` beats `--bg--stuck`), has no `--group-*` form, and `--stuck-state` takes no tiers: switch `--position` at a breakpoint to drop the state there. `<ui-debug>` flags an unknown side, a tiered `--stuck-state`, a `--*--stuck` on the sticky element itself, and a group form.
